// Generated by scripts/build-coin-availability.mjs from content-source/coins/descriptions. Do not edit by hand.
// NexuAlpha's own coin descriptions (en).
export const COIN_DESCRIPTIONS = Object.freeze({"a7a5":"A7A5 is a stablecoin pegged to the Russian ruble, launched in 2025 by a company registered in Kyrgyzstan and backed by ruble deposits at a Russian bank. It runs on Ethereum and TRON and is used for cross-border settlement. The token and its issuer have been placed under sanctions by the US, UK and EU, which affects where and how it can be used.","aave":"AAVE is the governance token of Aave, a decentralized lending protocol where users deposit crypto for others to borrow, or borrow against their own collateral. Holders vote on upgrades and risk settings and can stake AAVE to help cover shortfalls in the system. The project began in 2017 as ETHLend, and its original LEND token was swapped into AAVE in 2020.","aerodrome-finance":"AERO is the token of Aerodrome Finance, a decentralized exchange and liquidity hub on Base, the Ethereum layer 2 launched by Coinbase. Aerodrome went live in 2023 and builds on the Velodrome design: holders lock AERO as veAERO to vote on which liquidity pools receive new token emissions. Voters receive a share of the trading fees and incentives from the pools they support.","agora-dollar":"AUSD is a US-dollar stablecoin issued by Agora and launched in 2024. Each token is backed by cash, short-term US Treasury bills and overnight repurchase agreements, with reserves managed by VanEck and held in custody by State Street. It is used for payments, trading and DeFi, and is issued on many networks, including Ethereum, Sui, Mantle, Injective, Polygon and Monad.","akash-network":"AKT is the native token of Akash Network, a decentralized marketplace where people rent out and lease cloud computing, including GPUs used for AI training and inference. Akash is built with the Cosmos SDK; AKT is staked to secure the chain, used in governance, and accepted as payment for compute. Its open bidding model lets providers compete on price for each deployment.","akedo":"Akedo (AKE) is a token on BNB Smart Chain that belongs to the AI sector. The project positions itself around AI tooling, and AKE serves as its ecosystem token for participation and incentives.","algorand":"Algorand (ALGO) is the native coin of the Algorand blockchain, founded by MIT professor and Turing Award winner Silvio Micali and launched in 2019. It uses a pure proof-of-stake design in which block producers are chosen at random from among holders. ALGO pays transaction fees and secures the network, and the chain is often used for tokenized assets and payments.","ape-and-pepe":"Ape and Pepe (APEPE) is a meme token that combines two popular internet characters, an ape and Pepe the Frog. It runs as a token on the Polygon PoS network, where transfers are relatively cheap. Like most meme coins it has no core protocol function, and interest in it is driven mainly by its community and trading activity.","apenft":"NFT is the token of AINFT, formerly called APENFT, a project launched in 2021 within the TRON ecosystem. It started out aiming to bring well-known artworks on-chain as NFTs and to run an NFT marketplace, and the new name reflects a broader focus that includes AI services. The token is used across its platform and exists on TRON, Ethereum and BNB Smart Chain.","aptos":"APT is the native token of Aptos, a proof-of-stake layer-1 blockchain whose mainnet launched in 2022. The project was started by engineers who previously worked on Meta's Diem blockchain, and it uses the Move programming language for smart contracts. APT pays transaction fees, is staked by validators and delegators to secure the network, and is used in governance.","apxusd":"apxUSD is a dollar-pegged stablecoin issued on Ethereum, Base, BNB Smart Chain and Solana. It is designed to hold a value of about one US dollar and to serve as a stable unit for transfers, trading and DeFi applications on those networks.","arbitrum":"ARB is the governance token of Arbitrum, a group of Ethereum layer-2 networks built by Offchain Labs using optimistic rollup technology. Holders vote in the Arbitrum DAO on upgrades and treasury spending, while transaction fees on Arbitrum One are paid in ETH rather than ARB. The token was distributed by airdrop in March 2023.","arweave":"AR is the native token of Arweave, a blockchain built for permanent data storage that went live in 2018. Users pay AR once to store files, part of that fee goes into an endowment meant to cover storage over the long term, and miners earn AR for keeping data available. Arweave also hosts AO, a compute layer designed for running autonomous programs and AI agents.","aster-2":"ASTER is the token of Aster, a decentralized exchange focused on perpetual futures and spot trading that operates mainly on BNB Chain. The token is used for governance, incentives and fee-related benefits on the platform. It launched in 2025 and is held as a token on BNB Smart Chain.","avalanche-2":"Avalanche (AVAX) is the native token of the Avalanche blockchain platform, launched in 2020 by Ava Labs. AVAX pays transaction fees, which are burned, and is staked by validators under proof of stake. Avalanche supports Ethereum-compatible smart contracts on its C-Chain and lets projects launch their own custom blockchains; its maximum supply is 720 million tokens.","axie-infinity":"AXS is the governance and staking token of Axie Infinity, a blockchain game by Vietnam-based studio Sky Mavis in which players collect, breed and battle creatures called Axies. Holders can stake AXS and vote on community treasury decisions, and the token is also spent on in-game actions such as breeding. The game runs mainly on Ronin, an Ethereum-linked chain that Sky Mavis built for it.","backpack":"BP is the token linked to Backpack, a crypto exchange and self-custody wallet that grew out of the Solana ecosystem and is run by the team behind the Mad Lads NFT collection. It is issued as an SPL token on Solana and is meant to connect users with the platform's ecosystem and community programs. Its exact uses are defined by the Backpack team.","basic-attention-token":"BAT (Basic Attention Token) is an ERC-20 token tied to the Brave web browser, which blocks most trackers and ads by default. Users who opt in to Brave's privacy-respecting ads can receive BAT and pass it on to support websites and creators. The token launched in 2017 and has since been bridged to several other networks, including Solana and Polygon.","beldex":"BDX is the native coin of Beldex, a privacy-focused blockchain derived from the Monero codebase that launched in 2018. It hides transaction details with techniques such as ring signatures and stealth addresses, and its masternode network supports private messaging (BChat) and a decentralized VPN (BelNet). BDX pays transaction fees and is staked by masternode operators, and a BEP-20 version also exists on BNB Smart Chain.","bfusd":"BFUSD is a US-dollar-denominated asset created by Binance for use inside its own platform rather than on a public blockchain. It is mainly used as margin for futures trading, and its terms are set by Binance rather than by an on-chain protocol. Unlike a typical stablecoin, it is not designed to move freely between wallets.","bianrensheng":"币安人生 (BinanceLife) is a meme token on BNB Smart Chain whose Chinese name roughly means \"Binance life\". It became popular in 2025 among Chinese-speaking crypto communities as a playful nod to the Binance ecosystem. It has no protocol role, fee function or backing; its value depends entirely on community interest.","binancecoin":"BNB is the native coin of BNB Chain and is closely tied to the Binance ecosystem. It launched in 2017 as an Ethereum token and later moved to its own network, where it pays gas fees, is staked by validators and is used for governance. BNB has a scheduled burn program that permanently removes coins from circulation over time.","bitcoin":"Bitcoin (BTC) is the first cryptocurrency, launched in 2009 by the pseudonymous Satoshi Nakamoto. It runs on its own proof-of-work blockchain, where miners secure the network and confirm transactions, and people use BTC to send value and as a long-term store of value. Its supply is capped at 21 million coins, and the reward for new blocks is cut in half roughly every four years.","bitcoin-cash":"Bitcoin Cash (BCH) is a cryptocurrency created in August 2017 through a hard fork of Bitcoin. Supporters wanted larger blocks so the network could process more transactions at low fees and work better for everyday payments. BCH runs on its own proof-of-work blockchain, shares Bitcoin's 21 million coin limit and halving schedule, and is used mainly for peer-to-peer payments.","bitcoin-cash-sv":"Bitcoin SV (BSV) is a proof-of-work cryptocurrency that split from Bitcoin Cash in November 2018. Its supporters, including nChain, aimed to follow what they see as the original Bitcoin design, with very large block sizes for high on-chain throughput. BSV uses SHA-256 mining, has a 21 million coin cap, and is used for payments and data-heavy applications.","bitget-token":"Bitget Token (BGB) is the utility token of the Bitget exchange ecosystem. Holders can use it for trading-fee discounts and to join token launch events, and it is also the gas token of Morph, an Ethereum layer-2 network. In late 2024 Bitget merged its Bitget Wallet Token (BWB) into BGB and burned a large share of the supply.","bittensor":"TAO is the native coin of Bittensor, a decentralized network where participants provide machine-learning models and computing work and are rewarded based on how useful their output is. The network is divided into specialized subnets, and TAO is used for staking, registering on subnets and paying for services. Like Bitcoin, it has a 21 million maximum supply with periodic halvings of new issuance.","bittorrent":"BitTorrent (BTT) is the token connected to the BitTorrent file-sharing software, created after TRON founder Justin Sun's company acquired BitTorrent Inc. in 2018. BTT is used to reward users who seed files faster, to pay for decentralized storage on BTFS, and as the gas token of the BitTorrent Chain. The token was redenominated in 2021 at a rate of 1 old BTT to 1,000 new BTT.","bitway":"Bitway (BTW) is the token of a decentralized finance (DeFi) project. It is issued as a token on BNB Smart Chain and Ethereum, so it can be held in common wallets on either network.","blockchain-capital":"BCAP is a digital security issued in 2017 by the venture firm Blockchain Capital, representing an economic interest in one of its venture funds. It was one of the earliest tokenized investment funds and was created as a token on Ethereum. Because it is a security rather than a utility token, holding and transferring BCAP is subject to securities rules and investor eligibility requirements.","blockstack":"STX is the native token of Stacks, a smart contract layer for Bitcoin that was formerly called Blockstack. Stacks uses a consensus mechanism called Proof of Transfer, in which miners commit BTC to take part in block production and the chain settles to Bitcoin. STX pays for transactions and Clarity smart contracts, and holders can lock it through a process called stacking to help secure the network.","bnb48-club-token":"KOGE is the token of 48 Club, also known as BNB48 Club, a long-running community of BNB holders. The group operates a validator and related infrastructure on BNB Smart Chain, and KOGE is used for membership and governance within the community. It is a BEP-20 token issued on BNB Smart Chain.","bonk":"Bonk (BONK) is a dog-themed meme coin on Solana that launched in December 2022, when half of its supply was airdropped to Solana users, developers and NFT communities. It is neither a stablecoin nor a governance token; it serves as a community token, for tipping and inside Solana apps such as trading bots and games. Bridged versions also exist on networks including Ethereum, BNB Smart Chain and Arbitrum.","canton-network":"Canton Coin (CC) is the native token of the Canton Network, a blockchain designed for regulated financial institutions, with technology developed by Digital Asset. Canton lets separate applications keep their data private while still settling transactions with each other. CC is used to pay network fees and to reward the validators and application providers that keep the network running.","cardano":"Cardano (ADA) is a proof-of-stake blockchain launched in 2017 and developed with a research-driven approach by a team led by Ethereum co-founder Charles Hoskinson. ADA pays transaction fees, can be delegated to stake pools that secure the network through the Ouroboros protocol, and is used to vote on governance. The coin is named after Ada Lovelace, and its supply is capped at 45 billion.","celestia":"Celestia is a modular blockchain that specializes in data availability: it stores and publishes transaction data so that rollups and other chains do not have to run their own full consensus layer. TIA pays for posting data (\"blobs\") to the network, is staked to secure its proof-of-stake validators, and is used in governance. The mainnet launched in October 2023 and is built with the Cosmos SDK.","chain-2":"Onyxcoin (XCN) is the token of Onyx, a project that started as Chain, which launched XCN in 2022, before rebranding to Onyx. XCN is used for governance of the Onyx protocol and for fees within its ecosystem, which includes its own Onyx chain. The token also exists on Ethereum, Base and BNB Smart Chain.","chainlink":"Chainlink (LINK) is the token of a decentralized oracle network that delivers off-chain data, such as asset prices, to smart contracts. LINK is used to pay node operators for their services and can be staked to help secure oracle services. Chainlink also runs CCIP, a cross-chain messaging protocol, and its price feeds are used by many DeFi and tokenized-asset projects.","collector-crypt":"CARDS is the token of Collector Crypt, a Solana project that tokenizes graded physical trading cards, such as Pokémon cards, which are kept in professional vaults. Each card is represented by an NFT that can be traded on-chain or redeemed for the physical card. The CARDS token is tied to the platform's ecosystem, including its digital pack openings and community incentives.","compound-governance-token":"COMP is the governance token of Compound, one of the earliest lending protocols on Ethereum, where users supply crypto to shared pools or borrow against collateral. COMP holders propose and vote on changes such as which assets are supported and how interest-rate models work. Its supply is capped at 10 million, and distribution to protocol users began in June 2020.","conflux-token":"Conflux (CFX) is the native coin of Conflux Network, a layer-1 blockchain whose mainnet launched in 2020. It uses a Tree-Graph structure that processes blocks in parallel and combines proof-of-work mining with a proof-of-stake finality layer. CFX pays transaction fees, is locked as collateral for contract storage and is staked in PoS; the network also runs an Ethereum-compatible space called eSpace.","convex-finance":"CVX is the token of Convex Finance, an Ethereum protocol launched in 2021 that is built on top of the Curve exchange. Convex lets Curve liquidity providers and CRV holders earn boosted rewards without locking CRV themselves. CVX holders can lock their tokens as vlCVX to vote on how Convex directs its large share of Curve voting power, and receive a share of protocol fees.","cosmos":"ATOM is the native token of the Cosmos Hub, the first blockchain launched in the Cosmos ecosystem, in 2019. It is staked to secure the proof-of-stake network, pays transaction fees and gives holders a vote in on-chain governance. The Hub connects to many independent chains through the Inter-Blockchain Communication (IBC) protocol, which is why ATOM also appears on networks such as Osmosis and Kava.","crvusd":"crvUSD is a US-dollar stablecoin created by Curve Finance and launched in 2023. Users mint it by depositing crypto collateral such as ETH or wrapped bitcoin, so it is over-collateralized. Its distinctive feature is LLAMMA, a mechanism that gradually converts collateral as prices fall instead of liquidating it all at once. It is used across DeFi on Ethereum and several layer-2 networks.","crypto-com-chain":"Cronos (CRO), formerly known as Crypto.com Coin, is the native token of the Cronos blockchains, launched by the team behind Crypto.com. CRO pays gas fees on the Cronos EVM chain, can be staked to help secure the network, and is used for rewards and perks across the Crypto.com ecosystem. It also exists as an ERC-20 token on Ethereum.","curve-dao-token":"CRV is the governance token of Curve Finance, a decentralized exchange launched in 2020 that focuses on low-slippage swaps between similar assets such as stablecoins. Holders can lock CRV for up to four years to receive veCRV, which gives voting power, a share of trading fees and influence over which pools receive CRV emissions. CRV is bridged to many networks, including Arbitrum and Polygon.","dai":"Dai (DAI) is a decentralized stablecoin designed to stay close to one US dollar. New DAI is created when users lock crypto collateral worth more than the DAI they mint in smart contracts first run by MakerDAO, which has since rebranded as Sky. DAI is widely used across DeFi for trading, lending and payments, and it runs as a token on Ethereum.","dash":"Dash (DASH) is a cryptocurrency launched in 2014, first under the names XCoin and Darkcoin, built for fast and low-cost payments. Its network combines proof-of-work mining with masternodes, which enable features such as InstantSend for near-instant confirmation and CoinJoin-based mixing for extra privacy. Part of each block reward funds a treasury, and masternode owners vote on how it is spent.","decentraland":"MANA is the token of Decentraland, a 3D virtual world where users own land parcels as NFTs on Ethereum; it held its token sale in 2017 and opened to the public in 2020. MANA is spent on LAND, avatar wearables, names and other marketplace items, and holders vote on rules and grants through the Decentraland DAO. It is an ERC-20 token on Ethereum and is also bridged to Polygon.","decred":"Decred (DCR) is a cryptocurrency launched in 2016 that combines proof-of-work mining with proof-of-stake voting, where holders lock DCR in tickets to approve blocks and vote on rule changes. Part of each block reward goes to a treasury whose spending is decided by stakeholder votes. Supply is capped at 21 million coins, and the network offers optional privacy through its built-in StakeShuffle mixing.","derive":"Derive (DRV), formerly known as Lyra Finance, is a decentralized exchange for on-chain options, perpetual futures and structured products. It runs on its own rollup, Derive Chain, built with the OP Stack and settling to Ethereum. DRV is used for governance and staking, and stakers can receive benefits tied to activity on the exchange.","dogecoin":"Dogecoin (DOGE) is a cryptocurrency created in 2013 by Billy Markus and Jackson Palmer as a lighthearted joke, featuring the Shiba Inu dog from the 'doge' meme. It runs on its own proof-of-work blockchain using the Scrypt algorithm and is often merge-mined with Litecoin. DOGE is used for tipping and small payments, and it has no fixed maximum supply.","dogwifcoin":"WIF (dogwifhat) is a meme coin on Solana built around a picture of a Shiba Inu dog wearing a pink knitted hat. It launched in late 2023 and became one of the most widely recognized Solana meme tokens. It has no protocol function beyond being traded and shared by its community, and a version also exists on Unichain.","doublezero":"2Z is the token of DoubleZero, a networking project that gives blockchain validators access to dedicated, high-bandwidth fiber links instead of the ordinary public internet. Its first focus is Solana, where 2Z is issued as an SPL token. The token is used to pay for access to the network and to reward the contributors who supply bandwidth and infrastructure.","eigenlayer":"EIGEN is the token of EigenCloud, previously known as EigenLayer, a protocol on Ethereum that lets staked ETH be restaked to help secure other services. Those services, such as data availability layers, oracles and verifiable AI workloads, borrow Ethereum's economic security instead of building their own. EIGEN can be staked to back tasks whose faults are hard to prove on-chain, and it is also available on Base.","ethena":"ENA is the governance token of Ethena, a protocol that issues USDe, a synthetic dollar. Rather than relying on bank deposits, USDe is backed by crypto assets paired with offsetting short futures positions, which keeps its value close to one dollar. ENA holders vote on risk and protocol decisions and can stake the token; it launched on Ethereum in 2024.","ethena-usde":"Ethena USDe is a synthetic dollar launched by Ethena in 2024 that aims to stay close to one US dollar. Rather than being backed by bank deposits, it holds crypto collateral such as ETH and BTC and offsets their price swings with short futures positions, a so-called delta-neutral strategy. USDe is used in trading and DeFi and is available on many networks.","ether-fi":"ETHFI is the governance token of ether.fi, a liquid staking and restaking protocol on Ethereum. Users deposit ETH and receive eETH or weETH, tokens representing staked ETH that is also restaked through EigenLayer to help secure other services. ETHFI, released in 2024, lets holders vote on protocol decisions, and the project has also expanded into a crypto payment card.","ethereum":"Ether (ETH) is the native currency of Ethereum, a blockchain launched in 2015 that runs smart contracts and decentralized applications. ETH pays transaction fees (gas) and is staked by validators to secure the network, which moved from mining to proof of stake in 2022. Many tokens, stablecoins and DeFi apps are built on Ethereum, and part of every fee is burned.","ethereum-classic":"Ethereum Classic (ETC) is the original Ethereum blockchain that kept running unchanged after the 2016 split over The DAO hack. It still uses proof-of-work mining and supports smart contracts compatible with Ethereum tools. ETC pays transaction fees and rewards miners, and its supply is capped at roughly 210.7 million coins.","ethereum-name-service":"ENS is the governance token of Ethereum Name Service, the system that maps readable names such as alice.eth to wallet addresses and other records. Holders vote on proposals in the ENS DAO, which manages the protocol and its treasury. The ERC-20 token was airdropped to .eth name owners and contributors in November 2021 and has a maximum supply of 100 million.","euro-coin":"EURC is a euro stablecoin issued by Circle, the company behind USDC, and is backed 1:1 by euro-denominated reserves. It was launched in 2022 (originally as Euro Coin) and is issued under the EU's MiCA rules as an electronic money token. EURC is used for euro payments, foreign-exchange swaps and DeFi across Ethereum, Solana, Base, Avalanche and other chains.","falcon-finance":"Falcon USD (USDf) is a synthetic dollar created by the Falcon Finance protocol. Users mint it by depositing collateral such as stablecoins, BTC or ETH, with the system holding more collateral value than the USDf it issues. USDf can be used as a dollar-denominated asset in DeFi or staked in the protocol to receive sUSDf.","falcon-finance-ff":"FF is the governance and utility token of Falcon Finance, a protocol that issues USDf, a synthetic dollar minted against crypto assets, stablecoins and tokenized real-world assets as collateral. Holders can stake FF for benefits within the protocol and vote on its parameters. The token launched in 2025 and is available on Ethereum and BNB Smart Chain.","fetch-ai":"FET is the token of the Artificial Superintelligence Alliance, formed in 2024 when Fetch.ai merged its token with SingularityNET's AGIX (and initially Ocean Protocol's OCEAN). FET pays for services in the Fetch.ai network of autonomous AI agents, is staked to help secure the network, and is used in governance. It exists on Ethereum, Cardano, BNB Smart Chain and Cosmos-based networks.","filecoin":"Filecoin (FIL) is the native token of a decentralized storage network created by Protocol Labs, whose mainnet launched in 2020. Users pay FIL to store and retrieve data, while storage providers lock FIL as collateral and receive it for keeping files available. The network uses cryptographic proofs, such as proof-of-replication and proof-of-spacetime, to check that data is really being stored.","first-digital-usd":"First Digital USD (FDUSD) is a US dollar stablecoin issued by First Digital Labs, part of Hong Kong-based First Digital Group, and launched in 2023. Each token is intended to be backed one-for-one by cash and cash equivalents such as short-term US Treasury bills held in custody. FDUSD is used as a trading pair, for payments and for moving dollars across chains including Ethereum, BNB Smart Chain, Solana, Sui and TON.","flare-networks":"FLR is the native token of Flare, an EVM-compatible layer-1 blockchain whose token was distributed from January 2023, partly through an airdrop to XRP holders. Flare builds data tools into the protocol itself, such as the Flare Time Series Oracle (FTSO) for price feeds and the Flare Data Connector. FLR is used for fees, staking, delegation to data providers and governance.","floki":"FLOKI is a community-driven meme token named after Elon Musk's Shiba Inu dog and launched in 2021. Beyond its meme origins, the project has built products such as the Valhalla blockchain game and the FlokiFi DeFi tools, and holders can take part in governance votes. The token exists on both Ethereum and BNB Smart Chain.","frax":"FRAX, now labeled Legacy Frax Dollar, is the original stablecoin of Frax Finance, launched in December 2020 and designed to track the US dollar. It began with a partly collateralized, partly algorithmic model and later moved to full collateral backing. Frax has since introduced frxUSD as its main dollar stablecoin, which is why this earlier token is marked as legacy.","gatechain-token":"Gate (GT) is the token of the Gate exchange ecosystem, first issued in 2019 as GateToken. It is used for trading-fee discounts and platform events, and as the gas token of the exchange's own blockchain. Gate periodically buys back and burns GT, reducing the total supply over time.","gho":"GHO is a decentralized stablecoin issued by the Aave protocol and launched in 2023, designed to track the US dollar. Users mint GHO by borrowing it against crypto collateral deposited in Aave, so every unit is backed by more collateral value than it represents. Its borrowing rate and other parameters are set by Aave governance, and it is available on Ethereum and several other networks.","global-dollar":"Global Dollar (USDG) is a US dollar stablecoin issued by Paxos and supported by the Global Dollar Network, a group of crypto and fintech companies. Each USDG is backed by cash and cash-equivalent reserves and is designed to be redeemable one-for-one for US dollars. It is issued under Singapore's stablecoin framework and is available on networks such as Ethereum, Solana and Arbitrum.","gnosis":"GNO is the token of Gnosis, an Ethereum-focused project founded in 2015 whose early work produced tools such as the Safe multisig wallet and CoW Swap. Today GNO mainly secures Gnosis Chain, an EVM-compatible network that merged with the former xDai chain, where validators stake GNO to produce blocks. Holders also vote with GNO in GnosisDAO, which manages the project's treasury and direction.","grass":"Grass is a network in which users share their unused internet bandwidth through a browser extension or app, and that bandwidth is used to collect public web data for AI training. GRASS is the project's token on Solana, used to reward participants, for staking on network routers and for governance. The token was first distributed through a community airdrop in 2024.","grx-chain":"GRX is the native coin of GRX Chain, a project that runs on its own blockchain and is grouped with exchange-related tokens. Native coins like this are typically used to pay network transaction fees and for utility within the project's ecosystem.","hash-2":"HASH is the native token of Provenance Blockchain, a proof-of-stake network built with the Cosmos SDK and designed for financial services such as loans, securities and fund records. US fintech lender Figure Technologies led its creation. HASH pays network fees and is staked by validators and delegators, who secure the chain and vote on governance proposals.","hedera-hashgraph":"HBAR is the native coin of Hedera, a public network that reaches agreement using hashgraph consensus instead of a conventional blockchain. HBAR pays for transactions, smart contracts and file storage, and it can be staked to help secure the network. Hedera is overseen by a governing council of large companies and institutions and is known for low fees set in US-dollar terms.","htx-dao":"HTX DAO (HTX) is the token of the ecosystem around HTX, the exchange formerly known as Huobi. Launched in 2024, it is used for governance of the HTX DAO, where holders vote on proposals, and for benefits on the platform. Holders of the older Huobi Token (HT) could convert to HTX, which circulates on TRON, Ethereum and BNB Smart Chain.","hyperliquid":"HYPE is the native token of Hyperliquid, a layer-1 blockchain built around an on-chain order book for perpetual futures and spot trading. HYPE pays gas on the network, is staked to validators that run the HyperBFT consensus, and plays a role in governance. The token launched in late 2024, with a large share distributed to early users through an airdrop.","injective-protocol":"INJ is the native token of Injective, a layer-1 blockchain built with the Cosmos SDK for financial applications such as order-book trading, derivatives and tokenized real-world assets. INJ is staked to secure the proof-of-stake network and is used for fees and governance. Part of the protocol's fees goes into regular burn auctions that permanently remove INJ from circulation.","instadapp":"FLUID is the governance token of Fluid, the DeFi protocol formerly known as Instadapp; the old INST token was renamed FLUID in 2024. Fluid combines lending and a decentralized exchange on a shared liquidity layer, so the same deposits can serve both as collateral and as trading liquidity. Holders vote on protocol parameters, and the protocol runs on Ethereum and several other networks.","internet-computer":"ICP is the native token of the Internet Computer, a blockchain developed by the DFINITY Foundation that launched in 2021 and can host complete web apps and their data on-chain. ICP is converted into cycles that pay for computation and storage, and it can be locked in neurons to vote in the network's governance. Bridged versions also exist on Ethereum and Base.","iota":"IOTA is the native coin of the IOTA network, a project started in 2016 and supported by the Berlin-based IOTA Foundation. It originally used a block-less structure called the Tangle, aimed at IoT and data use cases, and in 2025 it moved to a new design known as IOTA Rebased with delegated proof of stake and Move smart contracts. IOTA is used for network fees and staking.","janus-henderson-anemoy-aaa-clo-fund":"JAAA is a tokenized share of a fund that invests in AAA-rated collateralized loan obligations (CLOs), a type of pooled corporate loan security. The strategy is managed by asset manager Janus Henderson and brought on-chain through Anemoy, an issuer linked to the Centrifuge real-world-asset network. Tokens exist on Ethereum, Base, Solana and other chains, and access is usually limited to eligible investors.","jasmycoin":"JASMY is the token of Jasmy, a Japanese company founded by former Sony executives that focuses on Internet of Things (IoT) devices and personal data. Its aim is to let people keep control of the data their devices produce and choose how it is shared. JASMY is an ERC-20 token on Ethereum used within this data platform.","jito-governance-token":"JTO is the governance token of Jito, a Solana project known for JitoSOL, a liquid staking token, and for the Jito validator client, which handles MEV, the extra value that comes from ordering transactions. JTO holders vote in the Jito DAO on fee settings, treasury use and protocol changes. The token was distributed through an airdrop in December 2023.","jupiter-exchange-solana":"Jupiter (JUP) is the token of Jupiter, a decentralized exchange aggregator on Solana that routes swaps across many liquidity sources to find efficient trade paths. JUP is used for governance through the Jupiter DAO, where holders vote on proposals and new launches. It was distributed in a large community airdrop in January 2024.","just":"JUST (JST) is the governance token of JUST, a decentralized-finance platform built on TRON and launched in 2020. Holders vote on proposals for its products, including the JustLend lending market, and JST is used to pay certain protocol fees. Part of the platform's income is used to buy back and burn JST.","kaia":"Kaia (KAIA) is a layer-1 blockchain formed in 2024 by merging Klaytn, launched by Kakao, and Finschia, launched by LINE, two networks linked to major Asian messaging apps. KAIA replaced the former KLAY and FNSA tokens and is used for transaction fees, staking and governance. The chain is EVM-compatible and targets wallets and mini-apps reached through messengers such as Kakao and LINE.","kamino":"KMNO is the governance token of Kamino Finance, a DeFi platform on Solana that combines lending and borrowing, automated liquidity vaults and leveraged positions in one interface. Holders use KMNO to vote on protocol decisions and can stake it within Kamino. The token was distributed to early users through an airdrop in 2024.","kaspa":"Kaspa (KAS) is a proof-of-work cryptocurrency built on a blockDAG, a structure that lets many blocks be created in parallel instead of in a single chain. This design, based on the GHOSTDAG protocol, allows very short block times and fast confirmations. KAS is used for payments and transaction fees, and the network had a fair launch in November 2021 with no pre-mine.","kinesis-gold":"Kinesis Gold (KAU) is a gold-backed digital currency where each token represents one gram of allocated physical gold held in vaults. It is part of the Kinesis monetary system, which shares a portion of transaction fees with holders and users as yield. KAU can be held, sent or spent, and the gold behind it can be redeemed under the platform's rules.","kinesis-silver":"KAG is a token issued by Kinesis in which each unit represents one troy ounce of physical silver held in allocated vault storage. It lets people hold, send and spend silver digitally, and the metal can be redeemed under the issuer's terms. Within the Kinesis system it is used for payments and holding value, and a version of it is available on Ethereum.","kite-2":"KITE is the native token of Kite, a blockchain focused on payments and identity for AI agents, so that autonomous software can hold a verifiable identity and pay for services on its own. Kite is an EVM-compatible network built with Avalanche technology, and KITE is used for staking, network security, governance and ecosystem incentives. The token is also represented on Ethereum, BNB Smart Chain and Hyperliquid.","kucoin-shares":"KuCoin Token (KCS) is the native token of the KuCoin exchange, launched in 2017. Holders can use it for trading-fee discounts and other platform benefits, and it is the gas token of KCC, the KuCoin Community Chain. KuCoin regularly buys back and burns KCS, with a long-term plan to reduce supply to 100 million tokens.","layerzero":"ZRO is the token of LayerZero, an interoperability protocol that lets applications send messages and tokens between different blockchains. Rather than being a standalone chain, LayerZero runs endpoint contracts on many networks, including Ethereum, Arbitrum, Base and BNB Smart Chain. ZRO was released in 2024 and is used for governance of the protocol.","leo-token":"LEO Token (LEO) is the utility token of iFinex, the company behind the Bitfinex exchange, launched in 2019. Holders can use LEO for reduced trading fees and other benefits on Bitfinex and related platforms. iFinex has committed to buying back and burning LEO over time, which steadily reduces the circulating supply. The token exists on Ethereum and on other networks.","lido-dao":"LDO is the governance token of Lido, a liquid staking service that lets users stake ETH and receive stETH, a token that stays usable in DeFi while it collects staking rewards. LDO holders vote in the Lido DAO on protocol settings, node operator choices and treasury spending. The token launched on Ethereum in late 2020 and is also bridged to several layer-2 networks.","lighter":"LIT is the native token of Lighter, a decentralized exchange focused on perpetual futures trading. Lighter runs as a zero-knowledge rollup on Ethereum, using cryptographic proofs to show that orders were matched and settled correctly. LIT is issued on Ethereum and also exists on Robinhood Chain.","litecoin":"Litecoin (LTC) is one of the earliest cryptocurrencies, launched in 2011 by Charlie Lee as a lighter, faster variant of Bitcoin. It runs on its own proof-of-work blockchain using the Scrypt algorithm, with new blocks about every 2.5 minutes. LTC is used mainly for payments and transfers, and its supply is capped at 84 million coins, four times Bitcoin's.","mantle":"Mantle (MNT) is the native token of Mantle Network, an Ethereum layer-2 network that bundles transactions and settles them on Ethereum. MNT pays gas fees on Mantle and is used to vote on proposals in its governance system. The project grew out of BitDAO, whose BIT token was converted into MNT in 2023.","memecore":"MemeCore (M) is a project built around meme culture that presents itself as a blockchain dedicated to meme coins and their communities. The M token belongs to the meme category and is available as a token on BNB Smart Chain.","meteora":"MET is the token of Meteora, a liquidity protocol on Solana whose pools are used by traders, new token launches and other DeFi apps. It is known for dynamic pools, including its DLMM design, which places liquidity in price bins and adjusts fees with market volatility. MET was launched in 2025 and is used for governance of the protocol.","midnight-3":"NIGHT is the native token of Midnight, a privacy-focused blockchain developed by the team behind Cardano and run as a Cardano partner chain. Midnight uses zero-knowledge proofs so applications can keep sensitive data private while still proving that rules were followed. Holding NIGHT generates DUST, a separate shielded resource used to pay transaction fees, while NIGHT itself is used for governance and block production.","monad":"Monad (MON) is the native coin of Monad, an Ethereum-compatible layer-1 blockchain that launched its mainnet in November 2025. It aims for high throughput by executing transactions in parallel and using its own MonadBFT consensus, while still running standard Ethereum smart contracts. MON is used to pay gas fees and is staked by validators and delegators to secure the network.","monero":"Monero (XMR) is a privacy-focused cryptocurrency launched in 2014 that keeps transaction details private by default. It uses ring signatures, stealth addresses and confidential transactions to hide senders, receivers and amounts. Monero is mined with the RandomX proof-of-work algorithm, designed for ordinary CPUs, and a small permanent block reward (tail emission) continues after the main supply was issued.","morpho":"MORPHO is the governance token of Morpho, a decentralized lending protocol on Ethereum and other EVM networks such as Base. Morpho lets anyone create isolated lending markets, while vaults managed by third-party curators spread deposits across them. MORPHO holders vote through the Morpho DAO on protocol upgrades and treasury decisions.","mx-token":"MX is the platform token of MEXC, a centralized crypto exchange founded in 2018. Holding or using MX can give users trading-fee discounts, access to new-token launch events and a say in listing votes on MEXC. It is issued as an ERC-20 token on Ethereum and also exists on Morph L2.","near":"NEAR Protocol (NEAR) is a proof-of-stake layer-1 blockchain that went live in 2020 and uses a sharding design called Nightshade to spread work across the network. NEAR pays transaction and storage fees and is staked to validators. The project has increasingly focused on AI and user-owned agents, and co-founder Illia Polosukhin co-authored the well-known 'Attention Is All You Need' paper.","neo":"NEO is the native coin of Neo, a smart-contract blockchain founded in China in 2014 as Antshares and renamed in 2017. Neo reaches consensus with delegated Byzantine Fault Tolerance (dBFT). NEO is used for governance, including electing the council that runs consensus, and holding it generates GAS, the separate token that pays network fees. NEO has a fixed supply of 100 million and cannot be divided.","nexo":"NEXO is the token of Nexo, a crypto lending and digital asset platform founded in 2018. The amount of NEXO a user holds on the platform sets their loyalty tier, which affects loan rates and other account benefits. It started as an ERC-20 token on Ethereum and is also available on several other networks, including Polygon.","non-playable-coin":"Non-Playable Coin (NPC) is a meme token named after the internet joke about people who behave like background characters in a video game. It launched on Ethereum in 2023 and was designed so that tokens and a linked NFT collection can be converted into each other. It has since been bridged to Solana, BNB Smart Chain, Base and other networks.","official-trump":"Official Trump (TRUMP) is a meme token on Solana linked to Donald Trump, launched in January 2025 shortly before his inauguration. It has no protocol utility such as fees or staking; it functions as a collectible and community token. A large part of the supply was assigned to affiliated entities and is released on a multi-year unlock schedule.","okb":"OKB is the utility token of the OKX ecosystem. It provides trading-fee discounts and platform benefits, and it is the gas token of X Layer, OKX's own layer-2 network. In 2025 a large token burn fixed OKB's total supply at 21 million, meaning no new OKB can be created.","olympus":"OHM is the token of Olympus, a DeFi protocol launched on Ethereum in 2021 that keeps a treasury of assets used to support the token. Olympus manages OHM's supply through buybacks and a range-based system, and holders can borrow stablecoins against staked OHM through its Cooler Loans feature. OHM also serves as the governance token of OlympusDAO.","ondo-finance":"ONDO is the governance token of Ondo Finance, a project that brings traditional financial assets such as US Treasury funds onto blockchains as tokens. ONDO holders take part in decisions about the Ondo ecosystem and its products. The token runs on Ethereum, and Ondo's tokenized products are aimed at both institutions and everyday on-chain users.","open-usd":"Open USD (OUSD) is a stablecoin designed to keep a value of one US dollar. It is issued as a token on several networks, including Ethereum, Solana, Base and Tempo, so it can be used for payments, transfers and as a dollar balance in on-chain applications.","optimism":"OP is the governance token of the Optimism Collective, which oversees OP Mainnet, an Ethereum layer-2 network that uses optimistic rollups to batch transactions and settle them on Ethereum. Holders vote in the Token House on upgrades, incentive programs and grants, while fees on OP Mainnet are paid in ETH rather than OP. The token launched in 2022, and the same OP Stack software powers a group of chains called the Superchain.","origintrail":"TRAC is the token of OriginTrail, which runs a Decentralized Knowledge Graph (DKG) for publishing and linking verifiable data, used in areas such as supply chains and AI applications. TRAC is spent to publish knowledge assets on the network and is staked by node operators who store and serve that data. It is an ERC-20 token on Ethereum, with a version on Base.","pancakeswap-token":"CAKE is the token of PancakeSwap, a decentralized exchange that launched on BNB Smart Chain in 2020 and has since expanded to Ethereum, Arbitrum, Base, Aptos and other networks. Users swap tokens against automated liquidity pools, and CAKE is used for liquidity incentives, staking and governance votes. Part of the platform's fees is used to buy and burn CAKE, reducing its supply.","pax-gold":"PAX Gold (PAXG) is a gold-backed token issued in 2019 by Paxos, a regulated US trust company. Each token represents ownership of one fine troy ounce of London Good Delivery gold held in professional vaults. As an ERC-20 token on Ethereum, it lets people hold and transfer gold digitally, including in small fractions.","paypal-usd":"PayPal USD (PYUSD) is a US dollar stablecoin issued by Paxos for PayPal and launched in 2023. Each token is backed by US dollar deposits, short-term US Treasuries and similar cash equivalents and is designed to be redeemable one-for-one. PYUSD can be sent and spent inside PayPal and Venmo, and it also circulates on blockchains such as Ethereum, Solana and Arbitrum.","pearl-2":"Pearl (PRL) is the native coin of its own blockchain and is listed in the AI category. As with most layer-1 coins, PRL is used to pay network fees and support the operation of the chain.","pendle":"Pendle is a DeFi protocol launched in 2021 that splits yield-bearing assets into two parts: a principal token (PT) and a yield token (YT), which can be traded separately. This lets users lock in a fixed yield or take a position on future yield. The PENDLE token is used for governance and can be staked or locked to receive a share of protocol revenue and to direct incentives.","pepe":"Pepe (PEPE) is a meme coin launched on Ethereum in April 2023, named after the Pepe the Frog internet meme. It has no formal roadmap or built-in utility beyond being traded and shared as a community token. Its fixed supply of about 420.69 trillion tokens was released at launch, and bridged versions exist on other chains.","pi-network":"Pi (PI) is the native coin of Pi Network, a project launched in 2019 that let people accumulate coins through a mobile app instead of energy-intensive mining. Its open mainnet went live in February 2025, after years in which balances could only be used inside the app's ecosystem. The network's consensus is based on the Stellar Consensus Protocol, and users generally complete identity checks before moving coins to mainnet.","pieverse":"PIEVERSE is the token of Pieverse, a project in the AI sector that builds infrastructure for AI agents, including on-chain payments. The token is issued on BNB Smart Chain and Ethereum and is used for participation in the Pieverse ecosystem.","plasma":"Plasma is a layer-1 blockchain designed specifically for stablecoin payments, especially USDT, with features such as zero-fee USDT transfers. It is EVM-compatible and anchors its state to Bitcoin for extra security; the mainnet beta launched in September 2025. XPL is the native token, used to pay transaction fees for other operations and staked by validators to secure the network.","polkadot":"DOT is the native coin of Polkadot, a network founded by Ethereum co-founder Gavin Wood that links many specialized blockchains to a shared security layer. DOT is used for on-chain governance, for staking under a nominated proof-of-stake system, and for obtaining blockspace for connected chains. Polkadot's mainnet launched in 2020.","polygon-ecosystem-token":"POL is the native token of the Polygon network and the upgrade of MATIC, which holders began migrating 1:1 in September 2024. POL pays gas fees on Polygon PoS and is staked by validators who secure the network. It is designed to also support other chains connected through Polygon's AggLayer.","pudgy-penguins":"PENGU is the token of Pudgy Penguins, a cartoon penguin brand that started as an Ethereum NFT collection in 2021 and grew into toys and media. Launched on Solana in December 2024, PENGU was airdropped widely to NFT holders and other crypto communities. It works mainly as a community and meme token, and is now available on several networks.","pump-fun":"PUMP is the token of Pump.fun, a Solana-based platform that lets anyone create and launch a meme coin in a few steps, with early trading running on a bonding curve. The PUMP token launched in 2025 and is linked to the platform's ecosystem and community. It is a Solana token, so it is held in Solana-compatible wallets.","pyth-network":"PYTH is the token of Pyth Network, an oracle that delivers market prices to blockchain applications. Its data comes directly from first-party publishers such as exchanges and trading firms, and the network started on Solana before expanding to many other chains. PYTH is used for governance and for staking that holds data publishers accountable for the quality of their prices.","quant-network":"QNT is the token of Quant, a company whose Overledger technology is designed to connect different blockchains with each other and with existing enterprise systems. Businesses and developers use QNT to pay for licences and access to Quant's network. The token runs on Ethereum and has a small capped supply of roughly 14.6 million QNT.","railgun":"RAIL is the governance token of Railgun, a privacy protocol for Ethereum and other EVM networks. Railgun uses zero-knowledge proofs so users can hold, send and swap tokens in a shielded balance without publicly exposing their address history. RAIL holders stake the token to vote on protocol changes and receive a share of the fees charged when assets move in and out of the privacy system.","rain":"Rain (RAIN) is a token from the decentralized finance (DeFi) sector, issued on Arbitrum One, an Ethereum layer-2 network. As an Arbitrum token, it is transferred and used within Ethereum-compatible wallets and apps, and network fees are paid in ETH rather than RAIN.","raydium":"RAY is the token of Raydium, an automated market maker (AMM) and liquidity provider on Solana that launched in 2021. Users swap tokens and add liquidity to pools, and many new Solana tokens get their first trading pools on Raydium. RAY is used for staking and liquidity incentives, and a portion of trading fees is used to buy back RAY.","re-protocol-reusd":"reUSD is a dollar-denominated token from Re Protocol, a project that connects on-chain capital with the reinsurance market, where insurers pass part of their risk to other firms. It is designed to keep a stable dollar value and is supported by reinsurance-related assets. The token is available on several networks, including Ethereum, Solana, Arbitrum, Base and Avalanche.","render-token":"Render (RENDER) is the token of the Render Network, which connects people who need GPU power for 3D rendering and AI work with node operators who have idle GPUs. Creators pay for jobs in RENDER, and operators receive it for completed work. The project was started by OTOY founder Jules Urbach, and in 2023 it migrated from Ethereum to Solana, replacing the older RNDR token.","ripple":"XRP is the native asset of the XRP Ledger, a public blockchain launched in 2012 and closely associated with the company Ripple. It is built for fast, low-cost payments and cross-border settlement, with transactions confirmed in a few seconds by a consensus process rather than mining. All 100 billion XRP were created at launch, and a tiny fee is destroyed with every transaction.","ripple-usd":"Ripple USD (RLUSD) is a US dollar stablecoin issued by Ripple and launched in December 2024. It is backed by US dollar deposits, short-term US Treasuries and cash equivalents and is designed to be redeemable one-for-one. RLUSD is issued natively on both the XRP Ledger and Ethereum and is aimed mainly at payments and institutional use.","safo":"SAFO is a tokenized share in a money-market style fund offered by Spiko, a Paris-based fintech, with the underlying fund managed by the asset manager Amundi. The fund tracks overnight interest rates through swap contracts, and the token lets eligible investors hold and transfer fund units on public blockchains. It is issued on several networks, including Ethereum, Arbitrum, Base, Polygon, Starknet and Stellar.","sei-network":"Sei is a layer-1 blockchain built for fast trading applications, with sub-second finality and parallel transaction processing. Since its v2 upgrade it runs a parallelized EVM, so Ethereum-style smart contracts can be deployed on it. SEI pays gas fees, is staked to secure the proof-of-stake network, and is used in governance; the mainnet launched in August 2023.","shiba-inu":"Shiba Inu (SHIB) is a meme token launched in 2020 by an anonymous creator known as Ryoshi, named after the dog breed popularized by Dogecoin. It started as an ERC-20 token on Ethereum and grew into a community ecosystem with a decentralized exchange, ShibaSwap, and a layer-2 network, Shibarium. SHIB has a supply in the hundreds of trillions, and part of it has been burned.","shuffle-2":"SHFL is the token of Shuffle, an online gaming platform that accepts cryptocurrency. It is an ERC-20 token on Ethereum and is used inside the platform for features such as reward programs and a lottery for token holders. Its role is tied closely to the Shuffle platform itself rather than to a separate blockchain or protocol.","sky":"Sky (SKY) is the governance token of the Sky Protocol, the 2024 rebrand of MakerDAO. Holders vote on risk settings and collateral for the protocol, which issues the USDS stablecoin alongside the older DAI. SKY is the upgraded form of MKR, with each MKR convertible into 24,000 SKY.","societe-generale-forge-eurcv":"EUR CoinVertible (EURCV) is a euro stablecoin issued by Societe Generale-FORGE, the digital-asset subsidiary of the French bank Société Générale. Each token is designed to be worth one euro and is backed by euro reserves held by the issuer. First launched on Ethereum in 2023, it is now also available on Solana, Stellar and the XRP Ledger, and is used for payments and settlement.","sofiusd":"SoFiUSD is a US dollar stablecoin issued by SoFi, the American consumer finance company that holds a national bank charter. It is designed to be redeemable one-for-one for dollars and backed by reserves held by the issuer, making it one of the few stablecoins issued directly by a regulated US bank. It runs on Ethereum and Solana and is aimed at payments and settlement.","solana":"Solana (SOL) is the native coin of the Solana blockchain, which went live in 2020 and is designed for high throughput and low fees. SOL pays transaction fees and is staked to validators that secure the network through proof of stake. A timekeeping technique called Proof of History helps order transactions, and Solana hosts many DeFi, NFT and payment apps.","sonic-3":"S is the native coin of Sonic, the network formerly known as Fantom, which relaunched under the new name with an upgraded chain in late 2024. FTM holders could convert to S at a 1:1 ratio. S pays transaction fees, is staked to secure the network and is used in governance; Sonic is EVM-compatible and lets app developers receive a share of the fees their apps generate.","spiko-amundi-overnight-swap-fund-eur":"EURSAFO is a tokenized share of a euro-denominated fund launched by Spiko, a Paris-based fintech, together with asset manager Amundi. Each token represents a fund share whose value aims to follow euro overnight interest rates, recorded directly on public blockchains. It is issued across several networks, including Ethereum, Arbitrum, Base, Polygon, Starknet and Stellar.","stable-2":"STABLE is the native token of Stable, a layer-1 blockchain built specifically for stablecoin payments, with a focus on USDT. On Stable, transaction fees are paid in USDT rather than in a separate gas coin, so everyday users can send dollars without holding another token. STABLE plays a different role, supporting network security and governance.","starknet":"STRK is the native token of Starknet, an Ethereum layer-2 network developed by StarkWare. Starknet is a validity rollup: it bundles many transactions and proves them to Ethereum with STARK cryptographic proofs, and its smart contracts are written in the Cairo language. STRK, released in 2024, pays transaction fees and is used for staking and governance.","stellar":"Stellar (XLM), also called lumens, is the native asset of the Stellar network, launched in 2014 by Jed McCaleb and the nonprofit Stellar Development Foundation. The network focuses on payments and issuing tokenized assets such as stablecoins, using the Stellar Consensus Protocol instead of mining. XLM pays small transaction fees and is required as a minimum balance for each account.","sui":"SUI is the native coin of Sui, a layer-1 blockchain built by Mysten Labs, a company founded by former engineers of Meta's Diem project. SUI pays transaction fees, can be staked with validators to secure the network, and is used in governance. Sui's mainnet launched in 2023, it uses the Move programming language, and the maximum supply is 10 billion SUI.","sun-token":"SUN is the governance token of SUN.io, a DeFi platform on the TRON blockchain that offers stablecoin swaps, liquidity pools and the SunSwap decentralized exchange. Holders can lock SUN to vote on proposals and to increase their share in the platform's liquidity mining programs. The project launched in 2020 within the ecosystem of TRON founder Justin Sun.","swissborg":"BORG is the token of SwissBorg, a Switzerland-based crypto investing app founded in 2017. BORG replaced the earlier CHSB token in a 2024 migration and is used inside the app to unlock higher membership tiers and other benefits. It is issued on Ethereum and Solana.","syrup":"SYRUP is the token of Maple Finance, an on-chain lending platform that provides loans to institutions and offers products such as syrupUSDC. It replaced Maple's earlier MPL token in 2024, with each MPL convertible into 100 SYRUP. Holders can stake SYRUP and take part in the protocol's governance.","terra-luna":"Terra Luna Classic (LUNC) is the native coin of the original Terra blockchain, which collapsed in May 2022 when its algorithmic stablecoin UST lost its dollar peg. After the crash, a new chain took the LUNA name, and the old chain and its coin were renamed Terra Classic and LUNC. The Cosmos SDK-based network is now run by its community, and LUNC is used for fees, staking and governance, including votes on token burns.","tether":"Tether (USDT) is a stablecoin issued by the company Tether since 2014 and designed to hold a value of one US dollar. The company says each token is backed by reserves held mostly in short-term US Treasury bills and cash equivalents. USDT is widely used for trading, payments and moving dollars between platforms, and it exists on many networks, including Ethereum, TRON and Solana.","tether-gold":"Tether Gold (XAUT) is a token issued by Tether in which each unit represents ownership of one troy ounce of physical gold stored in a vault in Switzerland. It lets people hold and move gold on a blockchain, including in small fractions of an ounce. XAUT runs as a token on Ethereum, and holders can look up the specific gold bars allocated to their address.","tezos":"Tezos (XTZ) is a layer-1 blockchain whose mainnet went live in 2018. It uses liquid proof of stake, where holders can produce blocks themselves (called baking) or delegate to a baker, and protocol upgrades are proposed and voted on-chain so the network can change without hard forks. XTZ is used to pay transaction and smart contract fees.","the-graph":"The Graph (GRT) is the token of a decentralized indexing protocol that organizes blockchain data into open APIs called subgraphs, which apps query instead of running their own data servers. Indexers stake GRT to provide this service, delegators assign GRT to indexers, and curators use it to signal useful subgraphs. Query fees are paid in GRT; the network's mainnet launched in December 2020.","the-open-network":"Gram (GRAM), previously called Toncoin, is the native coin of The Open Network (TON), a proof-of-stake blockchain whose design began at Telegram and is now developed by an independent community. The coin pays network fees, is staked by validators, and is used for payments and mini apps inside the Telegram ecosystem. Wrapped versions also exist on Ethereum and BNB Smart Chain.","the-sandbox":"SAND is the token of The Sandbox, a virtual world and game-creation platform owned by Animoca Brands, where players build, own and run experiences on digital land. SAND is used to buy LAND parcels and in-game assets, to pay platform fees, and for staking and governance votes. It is an ERC-20 token on Ethereum, with versions on Polygon and Base.","theta-token":"THETA is the governance and staking token of Theta Network, a blockchain launched in 2019 for decentralized video delivery and, more recently, AI and GPU computing through its EdgeCloud service. Theta uses two tokens: THETA is staked by validator and guardian nodes to secure the chain, while TFUEL pays for transactions and services. THETA's supply is fixed at 1 billion.","thorchain":"RUNE is the native asset of THORChain, a network that lets people swap native coins across blockchains, such as BTC for ETH, without wrapped tokens. Every liquidity pool pairs an asset with RUNE, and node operators must bond RUNE to help secure the vaults that hold users' funds. THORChain is built with the Cosmos SDK and began cross-chain trading in 2021.","tradable-na-rent-financing-platform-sstn":"This token represents a tokenized private-credit position: exposure to a North American facility that finances rent payments, issued through the Tradable platform. It runs on zkSync, an Ethereum layer-2 network, and is generally aimed at institutional and qualified investors rather than everyday trading. Its ticker, PC0000031, is a product code rather than a typical crypto symbol, and holding it is usually limited to eligible participants.","tron":"TRON (TRX) is the native coin of the TRON blockchain, founded by Justin Sun in 2017. TRX is used to obtain network resources, called bandwidth and energy, and is staked to vote for the 27 Super Representatives that produce blocks under delegated proof of stake. TRON is one of the most widely used networks for transferring the USDT stablecoin.","true-usd":"TrueUSD (TUSD) is a US dollar stablecoin that first launched in 2018 and is intended to be redeemable 1:1 for dollars held in reserve. It was originally created by the TrustToken team and is now issued by Techteryx, with reserve reports published through third-party attestations. TUSD is used for payments, trading pairs and DeFi on Ethereum, TRON, BNB Smart Chain and other networks.","trust-wallet-token":"TWT is the token of Trust Wallet, a self-custody crypto wallet app that Binance acquired in 2018. Introduced in 2020, it is used for in-app benefits such as fee discounts and for taking part in community features. TWT now lives mainly on BNB Smart Chain, after first being issued on the older BNB Beacon Chain.","unibase":"UB is the token of Unibase, an AI project that presents itself as a decentralized memory layer for AI agents, letting them store and share long-term data across applications. The token is issued on BNB Smart Chain and Ethereum and is used for access, incentives and participation in the Unibase ecosystem. As a newer project, details of its token model may still change.","uniswap":"UNI is the governance token of Uniswap, a decentralized exchange where people swap tokens directly from their wallets through automated liquidity pools rather than an order book. UNI holders vote on protocol changes and on how the community treasury is used. The token launched in 2020 with an airdrop to earlier users, and Uniswap has since added its own Ethereum layer-2 network, Unichain.","united-stables":"United Stables (U) is a stablecoin designed to hold a value of one US dollar. Like other dollar stablecoins, it is used for moving value between wallets and platforms, for trading, and as a stable unit in DeFi. It is issued on several networks, including BNB Smart Chain, Ethereum and TRON.","usa":"USAT is a US-dollar stablecoin designed to hold a one-to-one value with the dollar, available on Ethereum and Celo. Stablecoins like USAT are used to move dollar value on-chain, for payments and settlement, and as a stable asset in trading and DeFi. Reserve backing and redemption terms are set by the issuer, so it is worth reading the issuer's own disclosures.","usd-coin":"USDC is a US dollar stablecoin issued by Circle and launched in 2018. Each token is meant to be redeemable one-to-one for dollars and is backed by cash and short-term US Treasury holdings, with reserves reported regularly. It is used for trading, payments and DeFi, and it is issued natively on many blockchains, including Ethereum, Sui, Hedera and the XRP Ledger.","usd1-wlfi":"USD1 is a US dollar stablecoin launched in 2025 by World Liberty Financial. Each token is meant to be redeemable one-for-one for dollars and is backed by reserves of cash, short-term US Treasuries and cash equivalents, held with the custodian BitGo. It is issued on several networks, including Ethereum, BNB Smart Chain, Solana and TRON.","usdai":"USDai is a dollar-pegged token issued by the USD.AI protocol, which channels on-chain capital into loans backed by AI computing hardware such as GPUs. Holders can keep USDai as a stable unit of account or stake it in the protocol to receive a staked version tied to that lending activity. The token circulates on Arbitrum, Ethereum, Base and Plasma.","usdd":"USDD is a US-dollar stablecoin that started on the TRON network in 2022. It is designed to track one dollar and is backed by a reserve of crypto assets worth more than the USDD in circulation. It is mainly used for payments, trading pairs and lending within the TRON DeFi ecosystem, and is also issued on Ethereum and BNB Smart Chain.","usdgo":"USDGO is a stablecoin designed to track the US dollar at a one-to-one value. It is used like other dollar stablecoins: holding a stable balance, transferring value between wallets and serving as a unit of account in trading and DeFi. It is issued on Solana, Ethereum and Morph L2.","usds":"USDS is a US dollar stablecoin issued by Sky, the protocol formerly known as MakerDAO, and introduced in 2024 as an upgraded version of DAI; DAI and USDS can be converted one-to-one. It is generated against collateral held in the protocol, including crypto assets and real-world assets, and it circulates on Ethereum, Base, Arbitrum and Solana.","usdtb":"USDtb is a dollar stablecoin issued by Ethena, the team behind USDe, launched in late 2024. Unlike USDe, it is backed mainly by holdings in BlackRock's BUIDL tokenized US Treasury fund plus cash-like reserves, rather than by hedged crypto positions. It is used as a stable settlement asset and as part of the reserves supporting Ethena's other products.","useless-3":"Useless Coin (USELESS) is a meme token that launched on Solana in 2025 and openly presents itself as having no utility. Its appeal comes from the joke itself and the online community around it rather than from any product or protocol. A version of the token also exists on BNB Smart Chain.","usual-usd":"USD0 is a dollar stablecoin issued by the Usual protocol, designed to be backed 1:1 by short-term real-world assets such as tokenized US Treasury bills. Users can deposit USD0 into Usual's staking products to earn the protocol's USUAL token, and USD0 is used as a building block in DeFi lending and liquidity pools. It runs on Ethereum and several other EVM chains.","usx":"USX is a stablecoin issued on Solana that aims to keep a value of one US dollar per token. Like other dollar-pegged tokens, it is mainly used for payments, for moving value between applications and as a stable unit inside DeFi protocols. How the peg is maintained and what backs it are defined by the issuer, so it is worth checking the issuer's published reserve or collateral information before use.","vaulta":"Vaulta (A) is the native coin of the Vaulta network, the blockchain formerly known as EOS, which rebranded in 2025 and swapped EOS for A at a one-to-one rate. The chain uses delegated proof of stake and is positioning itself around Web3 banking and payment services. A pays for network resources and can be staked to help secure the chain.","vechain":"VET is the native token of VeChainThor, a public blockchain launched in 2018 that focuses on supply-chain tracking and enterprise use. VeChain uses a two-token model: holding VET generates VTHO, which is the token actually spent on transaction fees. The network has relied on a proof-of-authority style consensus with known, vetted validators.","venice-token":"Venice Token (VVV) is the token of Venice.ai, a privacy-focused AI platform founded by Erik Voorhees that runs open-source models without storing user conversations. Staking VVV gives holders a share of access to Venice's AI API capacity without paying per request. The token launched in January 2025 on Base, an Ethereum layer-2 network.","virtual-protocol":"Virtuals Protocol is a platform for creating and co-owning AI agents, mainly on Base, where each agent can have its own token. VIRTUAL is the base asset of the ecosystem: new agent tokens are paired with VIRTUAL in liquidity pools, and it is used to pay for agent services and launches. The project rebranded from PathDAO, a gaming guild, to Virtuals in 2024.","whitebit":"WhiteBIT Coin (WBT) is the utility token of the WhiteBIT crypto exchange, a platform founded in Ukraine. Holders can use WBT for benefits inside the WhiteBIT ecosystem, such as reduced trading fees and access to platform features. The token was first issued on Ethereum and TRON, and it is also used on Whitechain, the blockchain developed by the WhiteBIT team.","world-liberty-financial":"WLFI is the governance token of World Liberty Financial, a decentralized-finance project launched in 2024 and associated with the family of US President Donald Trump. Holders can vote on proposals about the project's platform and products, which include the USD1 stablecoin. The token was initially non-transferable and became tradable in 2025.","worldcoin-wld":"WLD is the token of World (formerly Worldcoin), a project co-founded by Sam Altman and developed by Tools for Humanity. It aims to give people a privacy-preserving digital proof that they are a unique human, called World ID, verified with an iris-scanning device known as the Orb. WLD is used for governance and ecosystem incentives and runs on Ethereum, Optimism and World Chain.","wormhole":"W is the governance token of Wormhole, a messaging protocol that lets blockchains such as Solana, Ethereum and many others pass data and tokens to one another. Cross-chain messages are verified by a network of 19 independent validators called Guardians. W launched in 2024 with a large community airdrop and is designed as a natively multichain token that moves between networks without wrapping.","xdce-crowd-sale":"XDC is the native coin of XDC Network, an EVM-compatible blockchain launched by XinFin and aimed at trade finance and tokenized real-world assets. It uses a delegated proof-of-stake system called XDPoS, in which masternodes produce blocks quickly and at low cost. XDC pays for transactions and is staked by masternode operators; this page's id refers to XDCE, the token from the project's early crowd sale.","ylds":"YLDS is a yield-bearing dollar token issued by Figure, built on the Provenance blockchain. Unlike most stablecoins, it is registered with the US SEC as a security and pays interest to holders, with its reserves held in short-term, high-quality assets. It is aimed at payments and settlement where users want a dollar-denominated token that also earns interest.","zama":"ZAMA is the token of the Zama Protocol, developed by Zama, a cryptography company known for its work on fully homomorphic encryption (FHE). FHE lets data be processed while it stays encrypted, which Zama uses to add confidential balances and private smart contracts to existing blockchains. The token is used to pay for protocol services and is staked by the operators who run the network.","zcash":"Zcash (ZEC) is a privacy-focused cryptocurrency launched in 2016 that runs on its own proof-of-work blockchain. It uses zero-knowledge proofs (zk-SNARKs) so users can send shielded transactions that hide the sender, receiver and amount, while transparent transactions work much like Bitcoin. ZEC is used for payments and has a maximum supply of 21 million coins.","zebec-network":"ZBCN is the token of Zebec Network, a payments project that started on Solana and is known for streaming payments, where funds flow to a recipient second by second, for example for payroll. The project also works on crypto cards and other links between digital assets and everyday payments. ZBCN is used for governance and staking in the ecosystem and replaced the earlier ZBC token."});
